<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <MSBuildAllProjects>$(MSBuildAllProjects);$(MSBuildThisFileFullPath)</MSBuildAllProjects>
    <HasSharedItems>true</HasSharedItems>
    <SharedGUID>57538685-8611-4019-89f0-9ca9efcd6f67</SharedGUID>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup Label="Configuration">
    <Import_RootNamespace>Birko.Data.Sync.Sql</Import_RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Models\SqlSyncKnowledgeItem.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Stores\SqlSyncKnowledgeStore.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Stores\AsyncSqlSyncKnowledgeStore.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- This project extends Birko.Data.Sync and a consumer must import
       Birko.Data.Sync.projitems alongside it.

       That used to be written as a <ProjectReference> to that .projitems, which MSBuild cannot honour:
       a .projitems is an include file, not a build target, and the path resolves against the CONSUMING
       project's directory rather than this one — so it emitted `warning MSB9008: The referenced
       project ..\Birko.Data.Sync\Birko.Data.Sync.projitems does not exist` in every importer, and an
       error under the -warnaserror the build sweep runs. It was inert: every consumer already imports
       Birko.Data.Sync explicitly.

       MSBuild has no way to express a shared project depending on another shared project, which is why
       this is a comment. Do not re-add the ProjectReference (TASK-238). -->
</Project>
